Evidence-Constrained Scientific Discovery: A Red-Team Theoretical PhD Proposal

Draft v0.7 — Dependence-Corrected Protocol Suite

Author: Ouadi Maakoul

Development Model: Human–AI Collaborative Research

AI: chatGpt, Deepseek, Meta AI , Gemini 


Abstract

This thesis proposes Evidence-Constrained Scientific Discovery (ECSD), a framework for the epistemically controlled transition from multimodal scientific evidence to persistent, falsifiable representations of scientific ignorance. The central research question is: Under what conditions can an observed discrepancy between multimodal scientific evidence and current knowledge be promoted to the status of a scientifically unresolved gap?

Draft v0.7 constitutes a dependence-corrected protocol suite. It does not report experiments or implementations. It specifies, step by step, how each component of the framework is constructed, calibrated, and falsified. The v0.7 revision addresses four residual technical vulnerabilities from v0.6: (i) the invalid product aggregation of e-values under spatial dependence; (ii) the mis-specified orientation and unconditional form of the dependency kernel K_{ij}; (iii) the confound between probe expressivity and physical alignment in the common observable space \mathcal{O}; and (iv) the near-tautological status of the FDR theorem. Each correction is formalized. The thesis is structured as a set of independently falsifiable contributions. The lunar surface serves as the demonstration domain. The core theoretical object remains an Epistemic Promotion Gate with explicit abstention: a mechanism that decides when an observed discrepancy is admissible as a persistent candidate of scientific ignorance, and refuses to decide otherwise.

