1. Introduction

1.1 Corrected Premise

Draft v0.3 claimed that "the representation problem is largely solved." Draft v0.4 weakened this to a domain-specific sufficiency claim. Draft v0.5 sharpened it. Draft v0.6 retained the corrected formulation. Draft v0.7 preserves it verbatim:

For the lunar demonstration considered here, sufficient open multimodal foundation models now exist to make construction of another foundation model unnecessary for the research objective.

The factual basis is as follows. Moonstone provides a 28-channel, 128 ppd global pretraining dataset with a modality-grouped masked autoencoder. Lunar-FM combines 18 data layers into a 768-dimensional embedding. The NASA/IBM Lunar Foundation Model integrates over 30 layers from 9 instruments, trained on approximately 2 million tiles, including 1 million high-resolution NAC images and 964k multispectral WAC images. These are sufficient for the present research. We do not claim that representation is solved in general; we claim it is sufficiently solved for this specific domain.

1.2 The Epistemic Question

The central question is not "How can AI discover scientific knowledge?" but rather:

How can a scientific AI system determine that its observations reveal something genuinely missing from current knowledge, under conditions of dependent evidence, spatial autocorrelation, and open-world uncertainty?

The core inequality remains:

\boxed{\text{Anomaly} \neq \text{Discrepancy} \neq \text{Knowledge Gap} \neq \text{Hypothesis} \neq \text{Discovery}}

1.3 Relation to Prior Work

AutoSciDACT (Bright-Thonney et al., NeurIPS 2025) establishes contrastive embeddings followed by NPLM two-sample testing to statistically quantify deviations in observed data relative to a reference distribution. SGHA (Gharat & Komiyama, 2026) structures a scientific literature corpus into evidence-linked paper objects and a typed evidence graph, detecting unresolved structural patterns across papers. HyGRAIL (Sun et al., 2026) generates hypotheses from incomplete scientific knowledge graphs with structured evidence validation.

The distinction is precise:

\boxed{\text{SGHA / HyGRAIL}: \text{knowledge-space gaps}}

\boxed{\text{ECSD}: \text{observation-space discrepancy} \rightarrow \text{knowledge-space gap}}

SGHA searches for holes in the structure of knowledge. ECSD determines whether a persistent empirical divergence justifies introducing a hole into the current representation of knowledge. This bridge — controlled empirical observation → discrepancy → knowledge insufficiency → falsifiable gap — is precisely where ECSD must prove its value.

1.4 Methodological Rule

\boxed{\text{Existing Work} \to \text{Reusable Components} \to \text{Overlap} \to \text{Unsolved Interface} \to \text{Formalization}}

1.5 Scope and Non-Implementation Clause

This thesis is theoretical and protocol-oriented. It does not report completed experiments, physical tests, or software implementations. For each component, we specify the construction protocol step by step, the calibration data required, the ground-truth benchmarks, and the falsification criteria. The thesis thus stands or falls on the coherence, identifiability, and falsifiability of the specified protocols, not on empirical results that remain to be produced.

