2. Theoretical Framework

2.1 The Epistemic Promotion Gate as a Sequential Filter with Abstention

The gate is a sequential filter with explicit abstention. Each gate c \in \{D, A, U, P, K\} produces a triple:

\text{gate}_c(x) = \left( \text{pass}_c(x), \text{conf}_c(x), \text{abstain}_c(x) \right)

where:

· \text{pass}_c(x) \in \{0, 1\} is the gate decision;
· \text{conf}_c(x) \in [0,1] is the calibrated confidence in that decision;
· \text{abstain}_c(x) \in \{0,1\} is the abstention flag, with the taxonomy defined in §3.6.

The promotion rule is:

G(x) = 1 \iff \forall c \in \{D, A, U, P, K\} : \text{pass}_c(x) = 1 \land \text{conf}_c(x) > \tau_c \land \text{abstain}_c(x) = 0

If any gate abstains or has confidence below \tau_c, the observation is not promoted and is instead placed in a deferred set \mathcal{Q}:

x \in \mathcal{Q} \iff \exists c : \text{abstain}_c(x) = 1 \lor \text{conf}_c(x) \leq \tau_c

The deferred set is a first-class output. An observation that cannot be decided is not a gap and not a non-gap; it is an open case. This preserves epistemic honesty and prevents silent false negatives.

Operational short-circuiting vs. benchmark parallel evaluation. In deployment, the gate is evaluated by short-circuiting upon the first failure, in the order D \to A \to U \to P \to K. In the benchmark protocol of Paper 2, all five gates are evaluated in parallel across the frozen N = 10{,}000 tile set, guaranteeing unconditioned marginal kill rates and permitting path-independence analysis. The distinction is specified in §3.4.

2.2 Three Levels of Output

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
S_1 &: \text{Statistical anomaly or discrepancy} \\
S_2 &: \text{Epistemically admissible unresolved discrepancy} \\
S_3 &: \text{Validated scientific discovery}
\end{aligned}}

The implications are asymmetric:

S_1 \not\Rightarrow S_2 \qquad \text{and} \qquad S_2 \not\Rightarrow S_3.

2.3 The Fundamental Principle

\boxed{\text{A discrepancy is not promoted to a knowledge gap unless independent epistemic controls are satisfied.}}

2.4 The Gap Object as a First-Class Artifact

The framework requires a formal schema for a gap candidate. A gap is not a scalar score; it is a structured record with a lifecycle. We define the Gap Record as follows.

\text{Gap} = \big( \text{id}, \; \Sigma, \; \Delta, \; U, \; \Gamma, \; \mathcal{E}, \; K, \; \text{KC}, \; \text{gate}, \; Q_{\text{FFSAP}}, \; \Pi, \; \sigma \big)

where:

Field Description
\text{id} Unique identifier
\Sigma Spatial–temporal scope (footprint, epoch, illumination regime)
\Delta Discrepancy vector (D_L, D_T, D_P) in the three-level hierarchy
U Uncertainty vector over the components \{U_{\text{sensor}}, U_{\text{calib}}, U_{\text{reg}}, U_{\text{temporal}}, U_{\text{model}}, U_{\text{epistemic}}, U_{\text{aleatoric}}, U_{\text{shift}}\}
\Gamma Provenance graph over evidence sources
\mathcal{E} Evidence set with per-item source attribution
K Dependence kernel matrix over \mathcal{E}
\text{KC} Knowledge Coverage score with component decomposition
\text{gate} Vector of (\text{pass}_c, \text{conf}_c, \text{abstain}_c) for each control c
Q_{\text{FFSAP}} Falsifiable question with locked pass/fail criterion
\Pi Validation plan (experiment, decoy, negative control)
\sigma Lifecycle status: \{\text{candidate}, \text{deferred}, \text{promoted}, \text{falsified}, \text{resolved}\}

The gap lifecycle is the object of study. A gap is not a knowledge claim; it is a tracked object of ignorance.

