3. Red-Team Corrections to v0.6

3.1 Correction 1: E-Value Aggregation Under Spatial Dependence Must Be Arithmetic, Not Multiplicative

Vulnerability in v0.6. In §3.3 and §4.4 of v0.6, tile-level e-values were aggregated to spatial block e-values by multiplication:

e_{\text{block}} = \prod_{x \in B} e_x

Why this fails. The product of e-values is a valid e-variable only under conditional independence or a multiplicative martingale filtration structure. Within a spatial block of size \geq \ell_{\text{dec}}, residual spatial autocorrelation — even if small — violates this requirement. Product aggregation of dependent e-values inflates the block-level rejection rate under the null.

Correction. Replace the product with a convex aggregation:

e_{\text{block}} = \sum_{x \in B} w_x \, e_x, \qquad w_x \geq 0, \quad \sum_{x \in B} w_x = 1

This is the standard arbitrary-dependence-safe e-merging function (Vovk & Wang, 2021). We use the uniform weighting w_x = 1/|B| for default deployments, and area-weighted or observability-weighted variants when tile coverage is non-uniform (see §4.2). The validity is proved in §5.1.

3.2 Correction 2: K_{ij} Must Be a Partial Distance Correlation with Correct Orientation

Vulnerability in v0.6. Two defects:

(a) Orientation error. v0.6 defined K_{ij} = 1 - \operatorname{dCor}(E_i \mid O, E_j \mid O) and stated that independent sources give K_{ij} \approx 0 and fully dependent sources give K_{ij} \approx 1. But if E_i, E_j are independent, \operatorname{dCor} = 0, so K_{ij} = 1; if fully dependent, \operatorname{dCor} = 1, so K_{ij} = 0. The stated interpretation is the reverse of the formula. Moreover, K_{ii} = 1 - \operatorname{dCor}(E_i, E_i) = 0, causing the effective-sample-size denominator to vanish.

(b) Unconditional measure. Standard distance correlation \operatorname{dCor}(X, Y) is an unconditional bivariate measure. Writing \operatorname{dCor}(E_i \mid O, E_j \mid O) implies conditioning on a continuous multi-dimensional observable vector O, which sample distance correlation does not natively support. The correct construct is partial distance correlation (Székely & Rizzo, 2014).

Correction. We redefine the dependency kernel as a partial-distance-correlation dependence matrix with the standard correlation-like convention:

K_{ij} = \operatorname{pdCor}_n(E_i, E_j ; O) \quad (i \neq j), \qquad K_{ii} = 1

The partial distance correlation \operatorname{pdCor}_n is computed via Hilbert-space orthogonal projection of distance matrices onto the subspace spanned by the physical observable O. The full computation is given in Appendix B. Under this definition:

· K_{ij} \to 0: E_i and E_j provide independent residual information once physical observables are conditioned out.
· K_{ij} \to 1: E_i and E_j share architectural or provenance dependence not explained by the physical state.

The effective sample size becomes:

N_{\text{eff}} = \frac{n^2}{\mathbf{1}^\top K \mathbf{1}} = \frac{n^2}{\sum_{i,j} K_{ij}}

For n mutually independent models K = I, giving N_{\text{eff}} = n. For n fully dependent models K = \mathbf{1}\mathbf{1}^\top, giving N_{\text{eff}} = 1. This corrects the orientation defect of v0.6.

Positive semi-definiteness note. Partial distance correlation is not guaranteed to be PSD in general. We therefore project K onto the nearest PSD matrix in the Frobenius norm prior to computing N_{\text{eff}}; the projection is reported as a diagnostic, and a large projection residual is treated as a calibration failure.

3.3 Correction 3: Probe Expressivity Must Not Be Confounded with Physical Alignment

Vulnerability in v0.6. v0.6 required a linear probe \hat{o} = W z + b with R^2 > 0.8 for a model to be considered physically comparable. This confounds model representation capacity with probe expressivity. Foundation models with deeply non-linear architectures (masked autoencoders, 768-dim Transformers) may represent physical reality on a non-linear manifold and still fail a strictly linear probe.

Correction. Replace the linear probe with a capacity-constrained non-linear probe:

\hat{o} = f_\theta(z), \qquad \|\theta\|_{\text{reg}} \leq C

where f_\theta is either:

· a kernel ridge regressor with Laplacian or Gaussian kernel and a fixed regularization \lambda; or
· a two-layer MLP with spectral-norm regularization on each weight matrix.

The complexity budget C is chosen so that the probe cannot memorize the physical ground truth: C is fixed by a nested cross-validation on the training block, and the same C is applied to all models. The comparability condition becomes:

R^2_{\text{probe}}(i, o) > 0.8 \quad \forall o \in O

for the capacity-constrained probe. The probe class is fixed across models, so differences in R^2 reflect differences in representational content, not differences in probe capacity.

3.4 Correction 4: Gate Ablation Must Be Evaluated in Parallel, Not Sequentially

Vulnerability in v0.6. The Gate Ablation Table was specified on a sequential filter. Sequential short-circuiting introduces path-order bias in the marginal kill rates: if gate U is evaluated first, it appears to kill more than if it is evaluated last.

Correction. The benchmark evaluation protocol in Paper 2 evaluates all five gates in parallel across all N = 10{,}000 tiles. Operational deployment retains short-circuiting for compute. The ablation table reports unconditioned marginal kill rates:

\text{Kill}_c = \frac{|\{x : \text{pass}_c(x) = 0\}|}{N}

\text{JointKill}_{c, c'} = \frac{|\{x : \text{pass}_c(x) = 0 \land \text{pass}_{c'}(x) = 0\}|}{N}

Path-independence is verified by permuting the gate order and reporting the maximum deviation of marginal kill rates across permutations.

3.5 Correction 5: D_P Must Be Observational, Not Merely Cross-Model

Vulnerability in v0.6. v0.6 defined D_P as a cross-model disagreement in the common observable space O. But cross-model disagreement in O-space can arise purely from probe bias, without any physical discrepancy. Two models may disagree in O-space while both are equally wrong about the Moon.

Correction. Distinguish three quantities:

D_P^{\text{model}}(x) = \max_{i,j} \|\hat{o}_i(x) - \hat{o}_j(x)\|_O

D_P^{\text{obs}}(x) = \min_i \|\hat{o}_i(x) - o_{\text{obs}}(x)\|_O

D_P^{\text{know}}(x) = \|o_{\text{obs}}(x) - o_{\text{expected}}(x)\|_O

where o_{\text{expected}} is the prediction from current knowledge (retrieved literature ranges, physical models, or calibrated background). Only D_P^{\text{know}} is a candidate for promotion. D_P^{\text{model}} is a candidate generator, not a discrepancy measure. D_P^{\text{obs}} is a consistency check on the probes. The promotion condition becomes:

D_P^{\text{know}}(x) > \tau_P \quad \land \quad D_P^{\text{obs}}(x) > \tau_P^{\text{obs}} \quad \land \quad D_P^{\text{model}}(x) > \tau_P^{\text{model}}

where the last condition ensures that the observation–knowledge discrepancy is not an artifact of a single probe.

3.6 Correction 6: Abstention Requires a Taxonomy and a Resolution Protocol

Vulnerability in v0.6. \text{abstain}_c(x) was introduced as a binary flag, but no taxonomy of causes was specified. This makes abstention a catch-all that can silently absorb false negatives.

Correction. We define the abstention taxonomy:

Symbol Cause Resolution protocol
\alpha_{\text{miss}} Missing modality in the input Impute or exclude; report separately
\alpha_{\text{ood}} Out-of-distribution input detected by density ratio Recalibrate on matched terrain; defer
\alpha_{\text{conf}} Gate confidence below \tau_c but above floor Collect additional evidence; defer
\alpha_{\text{conflict}} Retrieval evidence conflicts across sources Expert review required; defer
\alpha_{\text{undef}} Gate undefined for input class Human-in-the-loop; log and defer
\alpha_{\text{budget}} Computational budget exceeded Reduce tile resolution; defer

Each abstention event is logged with its type. The deferred set \mathcal{Q} is stratified by abstention type, and the deferred-set resolution rate is a first-class evaluation metric. A risk–coverage curve is reported for the full pipeline: coverage is the fraction of tiles not deferred, and risk is the false promotion rate among non-deferred tiles.

3.7 Correction 7: Knowledge Coverage Must Be a Calibrated Probabilistic Checker

Vulnerability in v0.6. The Knowledge Coverage score

\text{KC}(x, e) = w_s S + w_m M + w_p P + w_r R + w_q Q

was specified with weights calibrated by Bradley–Terry on expert annotations. This is an improvement over v0.5, but three problems remain: (i) retrieval recall is assumed perfect; (ii) the linear weighted sum imposes an additive structure that is not justified; (iii) the calibration mixes binary labels with pairwise comparisons.

Correction. Replace the weighted sum with a calibrated probabilistic classifier:

\Pr(\text{explained} \mid x, e) = \sigma\!\left(\beta_0 + \beta^\top \phi(x, e)\right)

where \phi(x, e) \in \mathbb{R}^d is a feature vector containing:

· S: max NLI entailment score across retrieved papers;
· M: mechanism-presence indicator from LLM extraction;
· P: predictive-interval coverage indicator;
· R: normalized replication score (not raw citation count);
· Q: scope-Jaccard overlap;
· \text{ret}_k(x, e): retrieval rank of e for query x;
· n_{\text{ret}}(x): number of retrieved documents above similarity threshold.

The KC checker is upper-bounded by retrieval recall: if the explaining paper is not in the retrieval pool, KC cannot exceed the recall ceiling. We therefore report a separate retrieval recall audit on the 100-case expert set. The knowledge insufficiency condition becomes:

\Pr(\text{explained} \mid x, e) < \tau_{\text{KC}}

with \tau_{\text{KC}} calibrated so that 90% of known-explained anomalies are not promoted.

3.8 Correction 8: The FDR Theorem Is a Conditional Proposition, Not a Deep Theorem

Vulnerability in v0.6. The "Theorem (False Gap Promotion Control)" in §5 assumed precisely the conditions needed for its conclusion: NPLM validity, conformal validity, disjointness, FDR validity, calibration, null specification, and provenance representation. This is not a theorem; it is a restatement of assumptions.

Correction. We downgrade the result to a conditional proposition with an explicit assumption set, and we add a simulation study to test robustness under mild assumption violations. The proposition is stated in §5.1 and the simulation protocol in §5.2. The simulation study is a first-class contribution: it tests whether the procedure controls FDR when block exchangeability is only approximate, when K is misspecified by up to 20%, and when the NPLM is trained on a shifted reference distribution.

3.9 Correction 9: Framework-Level Falsification Criterion

Vulnerability in v0.6. All falsification criteria were component-level. No criterion was specified for the framework as a whole.

Correction. We specify a framework-level falsification criterion:

ECSD Framework Falsification. If, on the frozen lunar benchmark, the set of promoted S_2 gaps has no statistically significant advantage over (a) a random-anomaly baseline and (b) a strong retrieval-augmented LLM baseline, in terms of predicting future confirmed observations on the held-out test set, then ECSD fails as a discovery framework.

The test statistic is the precision-at-k of promoted gaps for predicting future observations, with k \in \{10, 50, 100\}, evaluated against both baselines with a paired bootstrap test. A null result falsifies the framework.

3.10 Correction 10: Observability Model (Retained from v0.6)

The observability model is retained from v0.6:

O(x) = O_{\text{geom}}(x) \cdot O_{\text{SNR}}(x) \cdot O_{\text{coverage}}(x)

The Coverage Gap condition is:

\boxed{\text{Coverage Gap} = O(x) > \tau_O \land \text{Observed}(x) = 0}

with \tau_O = 0.9 as default. The observability model's false positive and false negative rates are validated against known observations before being used as a gate.

