4. Revised Formal Framework

4.1 Calibrated Discrepancy Detection

D_{\mathrm{cal}}(x) = -\log p_{\mathrm{conf}}(x)

where p_{\mathrm{conf}}(x) is the conformal p-value derived from the NPLM density-ratio score s(x) = f_{\hat{w}}(x), computed under strict train–calibration–test separation, geographic holdout, and block exchangeability.

4.2 Spatial E-Value Aggregation

For each tile x \in B, map the conformal p-value p_{\text{conf}}(x) to a non-negative e-variable via an optimal power calibrator with \kappa \in (0,1):

e_x = \kappa \cdot p_{\text{conf}}(x)^{\kappa - 1}

or via direct likelihood-ratio estimation e_x = p_1(x)/p_0(x). Truncate e_x \leftarrow \max(0, e_x). Define convex spatial weights:

w_x \geq 0 \quad \forall x \in B, \qquad \sum_{x \in B} w_x = 1

For uniform sampling w_x = 1/|B|; for non-uniform coverage w_x \propto \text{Area}(x) \cdot O(x) with normalization. The block-level e-value is:

e_{\text{block}} = \sum_{x \in B} w_x \, e_x

Multi-block FDR is controlled via the e-BH procedure on \{e_{\text{block}}^{(m)}\}_{m=1}^{M}.

4.3 Uncertainty Aggregation

Each uncertainty component is first normalized to a conformal p-value or calibrated nonconformity score U_i(x) = \text{normalized}(U_i^{\text{raw}}(x)). Then:

U(x) = \max\left(U_{\mathrm{sensor}}, U_{\mathrm{calib}}, U_{\mathrm{reg}}, U_{\mathrm{temporal}}, U_{\mathrm{model}}, U_{\mathrm{epistemic}}, U_{\mathrm{aleatoric}}, U_{\mathrm{shift}}\right)

The uncertainty gate is U(x) < \tau_U, with \tau_U calibrated at the 95th percentile of the reference distribution.

4.4 Evidence Dependence Control via Partial Distance Correlation

Define the dependence kernel K with K_{ii} = 1 and, for i \neq j:

K_{ij} = \operatorname{pdCor}_n(E_i, E_j ; O)

The effective sample size is:

N_{\text{eff}}(x) = \frac{|\mathcal{E}(x)|^2}{\mathbf{1}^\top K_{\mathcal{E}(x)} \mathbf{1}}

where \mathcal{E}(x) is the evidence set for x. The dependence gate is:

N_{\text{eff}}(x) \geq N_{\min}

with N_{\min} = 2 as default.

4.5 Knowledge Coverage

\text{KC}(x, e) = \sigma\!\left(\beta_0 + \beta^\top \phi(x, e)\right)

with \beta calibrated via L2-regularized logistic regression on the 100-case expert set. The knowledge insufficiency condition is \text{KC}(x, e) < \tau_{\text{KC}}.

4.6 Gap Promotion Criterion

G(x) = 1 \iff \forall c \in \{D, A, U, P, K\} : \text{pass}_c(x) = 1 \land \text{conf}_c(x) > \tau_c \land \text{abstain}_c(x) = 0

The deferred set is:

x \in \mathcal{Q} \iff \exists c : \text{abstain}_c(x) = 1 \lor \text{conf}_c(x) \leq \tau_c

Proposition (Evidence-Constrained Gap Promotion). Under the conjunction of the above conditions, x is promoted from S_1 to S_2. The promotion to S_3 requires independent experimental or observational confirmation and is outside the scope of the automated framework. The necessity and sufficiency of these conditions remain open.

