5. Statistical Results

5.1 Conditional Proposition: False Gap Promotion Control

Assumptions.

1. NPLM test statistic t_{\hat{w}}(\mathcal{D}) is valid under the null.
2. Conformal p-values are valid under block exchangeability within spatial blocks.
3. \mathcal{D}_{\text{train}}, \mathcal{D}_{\text{cal}}, \mathcal{D}_{\text{test}} are disjoint and geographically separated.
4. Block-level FDR correction is valid under positive dependence.
5. Uncertainty components are calibrated.
6. Null hypothesis H_0(x) is precisely defined.
7. Provenance dependence is correctly represented by K_{ij}.

Proposition. Under Assumptions 1–7:

\Pr(\text{false discrepancy promotion}) \leq \alpha

and for multiple candidates, \operatorname{FDR}(\text{false promoted gaps}) \leq q.

Remark. This is a conditional statement, not a theorem about scientific discovery. ECSD is not a machine that discovers truth; it is a controlled mechanism for deciding when an observed discrepancy is admissible as a persistent candidate of scientific ignorance.

5.2 Theorem: E-Value Aggregation Under Arbitrary Spatial Dependence

Theorem. Let B be a bounded spatial block containing |B| tiles. Let H_0(x) denote the null hypothesis at tile x, and let e_x \geq 0 satisfy \mathbb{E}_{H_0(x)}[e_x] \leq 1. Let H_0^B = \bigcap_{x \in B} H_0(x). For any convex weights \{w_x\}_{x \in B} with w_x \geq 0 and \sum_x w_x = 1, the aggregated variable

e_{\text{block}} = \sum_{x \in B} w_x \, e_x

is a valid e-variable under H_0^B, satisfying \mathbb{E}_{H_0^B}[e_{\text{block}}] \leq 1, for any arbitrary joint spatial dependence structure.

Proof. Non-negativity is immediate. By linearity of expectation under P_{H_0^B}:

\mathbb{E}_{H_0^B}[e_{\text{block}}] = \sum_{x \in B} w_x \mathbb{E}_{H_0^B}[e_x].

Under H_0^B, each marginal H_0(x) holds, so \mathbb{E}_{H_0^B}[e_x] = \mathbb{E}_{H_0(x)}[e_x] \leq 1. Substituting:

\mathbb{E}_{H_0^B}[e_{\text{block}}] \leq \sum_{x \in B} w_x \cdot 1 = 1. \qquad \blacksquare

Corollary (Type I error control). By Markov's inequality, for any \alpha \in (0,1):

\mathbb{P}_{H_0^B}\!\left(e_{\text{block}} \geq \alpha^{-1}\right) \leq \alpha.

This guarantee requires no assumption of spatial decorrelation length, isotropy, or Gaussianity.

5.3 Simulation Study Protocol

The simulation study tests robustness under mild assumption violations. Four scenarios are evaluated:

Scenario Violation Severity
S1 Block exchangeability approximately holds Moderate residual autocorrelation (Moran's I ≈ 0.15)
S2 K misspecified by up to 20% Perturbation of off-diagonal entries
S3 NPLM trained on shifted reference Covariate shift in reference distribution
S4 Uncertainty calibration drift \tau_U mis-set by ±10%

For each scenario, the empirical FDR and power are estimated over 1,000 synthetic replications. The proposition is considered robust if empirical FDR remains below q + 0.05 in all scenarios and power loss is below 20% relative to the idealized setting.

