6. Falsification Protocol

6.1 FFSAP Contract

Every generated question Q must be convertible into a locked, measurable claim with hidden validation, decoys, and negative controls. A question is FFSAP-compliant if it specifies a pass/fail criterion evaluable by a predetermined experiment.

6.2 Retrieval-Dependence Audit

Protocol.

1. Identify the set \mathcal{P}(H) of papers that contain hypothesis H or its direct entailments.
2. Apply unlearning to remove \mathcal{P}(H) from the model's training distribution.
3. Verify that unlearning actually removed \mathcal{P}(H).
4. Re-prompt the model with the same gap G and question Q.
5. Measure whether H is regenerated.
6. Repeat across multiple unlearning methods, seeds, and prompts.
7. Include control hypotheses known to be in the training data and control gaps that should not regenerate.

Interpretation. Regeneration after unlearning indicates constructive dependence on the gap and question, not on the specific paper. Failure to regenerate indicates retrieval dependence. Neither outcome proves "originality."

6.3 Framework-Level Falsification Criterion

See §3.9. The frozen benchmark is pre-registered; the baselines (random anomaly, retrieval-augmented LLM) are specified in advance; the test statistic is precision-at-k for future observations; the test is a paired bootstrap at \alpha = 0.05.

6.4 Evaluation Metrics

Metric Definition
Gap precision P_G validated gaps / proposed gaps
Gap recall R_G benchmark gaps recovered / known gaps
FDR false gaps / all gaps (block-level corrected)
Evidence coverage claims with traceable evidence / total claims
Provenance completeness evidence with complete provenance / total evidence
Question quality binary FFSAP compliance
Retrieval dependence P(H \text{ regenerated} \mid \text{unlearning})
N_{\text{eff}} calibration correlation with human-annotated evidence independence
Abstention rate deferred cases / total cases
Deferred resolution rate resolved deferrals / total deferrals
Risk–coverage AUC area under risk–coverage curve

