Appendix G — Knowledge Coverage Calibration

G.1 Expert Set Construction

The 100-case expert set is constructed by stratified sampling across gap types (inconsistency, boundary, coverage) and across observables (albedo, $T_b$, CPR, neutron count). Each case is annotated by two independent domain experts with a binary label $\{\text{explained}, \text{unexplained}\}$ and a confidence score. Disagreements are resolved by a third expert. Inter-annotator agreement is reported (Cohen's $\kappa$).

G.2 Feature Vector

\phi(x, e) = \left(S, M, P, R, Q, n_{\text{ret}}(x)\right)^\top \in \mathbb{R}^6.

The retrieval rank $\text{ret}_k(x, e)$ is excluded to avoid circularity (§3.6).

G.3 Calibration

The classifier is:

\Pr(\text{explained} \mid x, e) = \sigma\!\left(\beta_0 + \beta^\top \phi(x, e)\right).

$\beta$ is fit by L2-regularized logistic regression with 5-fold cross-validation on the 100-case set. Regularization strength is selected by nested CV on the inner folds.

G.4 Hierarchical Robustness Check

Given the small sample size, a hierarchical model is fit as a robustness check:

\beta_k \sim \mathcal{N}(\beta_{\text{global}}, \sigma^2_\beta), \quad k \in \{\text{gap types}\}.

The hierarchical model pools information across gap types, reducing variance at the cost of potential bias. The two fits are compared on a held-out 20-case set.

G.5 Reliability Diagram and Brier Score

The calibration is reported as a reliability diagram (predicted probability vs. observed frequency in bins) and a Brier score:

\operatorname{BS} = \frac{1}{100} \sum_{i=1}^{100} \left(\hat{p}_i - y_i\right)^2.

A well-calibrated classifier has $\operatorname{BS} \leq \operatorname{BS}_{\text{baseline}} - 0.05$, where $\operatorname{BS}_{\text{baseline}}$ is the Brier score of the constant predictor equal to the base rate.

G.6 Retrieval Recall Ceiling

The retrieval recall ceiling is:

\operatorname{RC} = \frac{|\{x : e^*(x) \in \mathcal{P}_{\text{ret}}(x)\}|}{|\{x\}|},

where $e^*(x)$ is the true explaining paper (if it exists) and $\mathcal{P}_{\text{ret}}(x)$ is the retrieval pool. $\operatorname{RC}$ is estimated on the 100-case set. The KC checker is upper-bounded by $\operatorname{RC}$:

\text{KC}(x, e) \leq \operatorname{RC} \quad \text{for all } x.

A separate retrieval recall audit reports $\operatorname{RC}$ per gap type and per observable.

