Appendix K — Retrieval-Dependence Audit: Extended Protocol

K.1 Unlearning Methods

Method Description Verification
Gradient ascent Maximize loss on $\mathcal{P}(H)$ Canary insertion
Influence unlearning Remove influence of $\mathcal{P}(H)$ from weights Membership inference
SISA Sharded, isolated, sliced, aggregated retraining Held-out perplexity
Prompt suppression In-context suppression of $\mathcal{P}(H)$ Prompt-level canary

K.2 Verification

For each method, the false-negative rate of verification is:

\operatorname{FNR}_{\text{ver}} = \frac{|\{H : \text{unlearning failed}, \text{verification says success}\}|}{|\{H : \text{unlearning failed}\}|}.

The FNR bounds the interpretability of the regeneration test. If $\operatorname{FNR}_{\text{ver}} > 0.20$, the audit is inconclusive.

K.3 Regeneration Test

After verified unlearning, re-prompt the model with the gap $G$ and question $Q$. Regeneration is measured by:

\text{Regenerate}(H) = \mathbb{1}\left[\text{NLI}(H, \text{model output}) > 0.8\right].

The retrieval-dependence score is:

\operatorname{RD}(H) = 1 - \frac{1}{n_{\text{runs}}} \sum_{r=1}^{n_{\text{runs}}} \text{Regenerate}_r(H).

High $\operatorname{RD}$ indicates retrieval dependence; low $\operatorname{RD}$ indicates constructive generation. Neither proves originality.

K.4 Controls

· Positive control: $H$ known to be in the training data. Expected: $\operatorname{RD} \approx 0$.
· Negative control: $H$ known to be absent from the training data and not derivable. Expected: $\operatorname{RD} \approx 1$.
· Null control: $H$ random. Expected: $\operatorname{RD} \approx 1$.

The audit is valid if the positive and null controls yield the expected outcomes.

